@page "/components/ai/messagepartview"

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/messagepartview"
            Title="AiMessagePartView"
            Description="BitAiMessagePartView renders one part of an AI message with the view of its kind - text, reasoning, code, a tool call, an error - or with a renderer the app registered, and re-renders only when that part changes." />

<DemoPage Name="AiMessagePartView"
          Description="BitAiMessagePartView renders one part of a message with the view of its kind: Markdown text, a reasoning panel, a code block, a tool call card, a tool result, an image, a file chip, an artifact card, structured data, a generated component, a source list, the card of a citation or an error. It listens to its part rather than to the message, so a token arriving in the last paragraph of a long answer re-renders that paragraph alone, at most once per render interval. A renderer the app registers for a part kind replaces the built-in view, inside a boundary that contains its failures; a kind no view knows is shown as the text it carries."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="MessagePartView/BitAiMessagePartView.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/MessagePartView/BitAiMessagePartViewDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/chatmessage">BitAiChatMessage</BitLink> renders every part of a message through this view; render it
            yourself to lay the parts of a message out your own way. It is the extension point for content the library has no view for: give
            the part a kind of its own (<b>BitAiCustomPart&lt;T&gt;</b>, <b>BitAiCustomJsonPart</b>) and register a renderer for that kind with
            <b>BitAiRendererRegistry</b> at startup. A registered renderer is handed what the model produced, so it is rendered inside a
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/rendererboundary">BitAiRendererBoundary</BitLink>: a shape it did not expect replaces that one part with
            a notice. See the <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/streaming">Streaming</BitLink>,
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/custom-tool">Custom tool UI</BitLink> and
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/performance">Performance</BitLink> scenarios.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Hand it a <b>Part</b> and the <b>Message</b> it belongs to, and render one per part of the message: each part is drawn by the
                view of its kind - here two text parts around a tool call.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-message">
                @foreach (var part in basicMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Part="part" Message="basicMessage" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Streaming" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                Each view listens to its own part. As the answer below streams in, its text part is the only thing that re-renders - at most once
                per <b>RenderInterval</b> of the options, however fast the tokens come - while the page, and the tool card above, are left alone.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="StreamAsync" Disabled="isStreaming">Stream an answer</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            <div class="example-message">
                @foreach (var part in streamingMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Part="part" Message="streamingMessage" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Part kinds" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                A reasoning part is a collapsed panel, a code part a code block, a tool result a card of its own and an error part a compact error
                in the flow of the answer - see the BitAiMessagePartKinds table for every kind. A part of a kind no view knows and no renderer is
                registered for is shown as the <b>Text</b> it carries, rather than dropped.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-message">
                @foreach (var part in kindsMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Part="part" Message="kindsMessage" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Citations" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                The bracketed numbers of a text part are chips for the citations of its message (<b>BitAiMessage.AddCitation</b> numbers them).
                A <b>BitAiCitationPart</b> places one citation where it sits between the parts instead: the card of its source, numbered as
                the message numbers it, with the <b>Quote</b> and the <b>Locator</b> of the citation under it. <b>OnCitationClick</b> reports
                the one the user activates, a chip or the card; while it is not set, the card is a link to the source.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-message">
                @foreach (var part in citedMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Part="part" Message="citedMessage" OnCitationClick="c => clickedCitation = c.Source.Title" />
                }
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>Activated: <b>@(clickedCitation ?? "none yet")</b></div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Custom part renderer" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example5CodeFiles" Id="example5">
            <div>
                A renderer registered for a kind (<b>BitAiRendererRegistry.RegisterPart</b>, see the tables below) replaces the built-in view of
                every part of that kind. Here an invoice the model produced travels as a <b>BitAiCustomJsonPart</b> of the kind
                <b>demo:invoice</b>, and an <b>InvoiceCard</b> component is registered for it: the registry renders it with the part as its
                <b>Part</b> parameter. <b>RegisterPart(kind, context => builder => ...)</b> takes a <b>BitAiMessagePartRenderer</b> delegate
                instead, handed the part, its message, the conversation and the session. An app registers its renderers once, at startup
                (the Program.cs tab); this page registers its own kind as it loads instead.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-message">
                @foreach (var part in invoiceMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Part="part" Message="invoiceMessage" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Renderer failure" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                A registered renderer is handed what a model produced, so the view renders it inside a
                <BitLink Href="/components/ai/rendererboundary">BitAiRendererBoundary</BitLink>. The second invoice below has no <b>lines</b>,
                which the card was not written for: it throws, and that one part is replaced by a notice naming the kind whose renderer failed
                (point at it for the exception). The rest of the message is unaffected.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-message">
                @foreach (var part in brokenMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Part="part" Message="brokenMessage" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the root, the block each part is laid out in; <b>Styles.Fallback</b> and
                <b>Classes.Fallback</b> on the text shown for a kind no view knows; <b>Citation</b>, <b>CitationCard</b>,
                <b>CitationQuote</b> and <b>CitationLocator</b> on what a citation part shows, which the public
                <b>--bit-AiMessagePartView-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table) restyle too. What any other part shows is drawn
                by the view of its kind, restyled through that view's own parameters and variables.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-message">
                <BitAiMessagePartView Part="styleMessage.Parts[0]" Message="styleMessage" Style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; border-radius: 0.5rem; color: #1e1b4b; background-color: #f5f3ff" />

                <BitAiMessagePartView Part="styleMessage.Parts[0]" Message="styleMessage" Class="custom-part" />

                <BitAiMessagePartView Part="styleMessage.Parts[1]" Message="styleMessage" Styles="@(new() { Fallback = "font-style: italic; color: #6d28d9" })" />

                <BitAiMessagePartView Part="styleMessage.Parts[1]" Message="styleMessage" Classes="@(new() { Root = "custom-part-root", Fallback = "custom-part-fallback" })" />

                <BitAiMessagePartView Part="styleMessage.Parts[2]" Message="styleMessage" Styles="@(new() { CitationQuote = "font-style: normal; color: #6d28d9", CitationLocator = "font-weight: bold" })" />

                <div style="--bit-AiMessagePartView-citation-gap: 0.25rem; --bit-AiMessagePartView-quote-color: #0b7a75; --bit-AiMessagePartView-quote-border-color: #0b7a75; --bit-AiMessagePartView-locator-color: #0b7a75">
                    <BitAiMessagePartView Part="styleMessage.Parts[2]" Message="styleMessage" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>Use BitAiMessagePartView in right-to-left (RTL).</div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-message">
                @foreach (var part in rtlMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Part="part" Message="rtlMessage" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
